You can contact me for any questions.\par
My English is not very good, and if I have any translation errors, you can write me :)\par
\par
\par
Package includes 17 prefabs of the blood effects (blood mesh + decal) and prefab for shot point decal.\par
Support platforms: all platforms (PC/Consoles/VR/Mobiles/URP/HDRP)\par
\par
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\par
\cf2 IMPORTANT\par
\cf0\par
\cf2 For all platforms \cf0 required format "RGBA half" or compressed "RGB9e5 23 bit Shared Exponent Float" for textures with the name "blood_norm" and "blood_pos"\par
RGB9e5 this is a lossy format so the blood drops will be sharper.\par
\par
\cf2 For mobile devices \cf0 you must to do "texture overide for android -> RGBA half/RGB9e5", because by default unity used RGBM encoding for android and this format does not work correctly.\par
\par
\cf2 For HDRP and URP \cf0 you need to import patches from the folder "\\Assets\\KriptoFX\\VolumetricBloodFX\\URP and HDRP Patches"\par
\par
\cf2 For correct rendering of screen space decals\cf3  \cf0 you need:\par
    -For STANDARD FORWARD: MainCamera -> Add the script "BFX_RenderDepth"\par
    -For URP rendering mainCamera -> DepthTexture = ON\par
    -For HDRP depth rendering works by default\par
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\par
\ul\b Effects using:\par
\par
\cf4\ulnone\b0\f1\lang1049 1\b ) \ul Insantiate effect\ulnone :\par
\cf5\b0 The blood should be rotated to the right angle.\par
Since the animation is baked and gravity must flow down. If you turn the effect up (for example) then the gravity animation will flow left/right.\par
\par
var direction = .... //you must assign the direction, for example Physics.Raycast(ray, out hit); direction = hit.normal;\par
float angle = Mathf.Atan2(direction.x, direction.z) * Mathf.Rad2Deg + 180;\par
var instance = Instantiate(bloodPrefab, hit.point, Quaternion.Euler(0, angle + 90, 0));\par
\par
\cf4 2) \ul\b Set the start height position \cf5\ulnone\b0 for decal. It's your terrain/floor transform position Y\par
\par
instance.GetComponent<BFX_BloodSettings>().GroundHeight = ...\par
\cf0\f0\lang9 ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\par
\ul\b Effect optimisations:\par
\ulnone\b0\par
1) You can \b change texture format \b0 for textures with the name "blood_norm" and "blood_pos".\par
Click on current platform (not a "default" tab!) -> override for "current platform name" -> select format "RGB9e5 23 bit Shared Exponent Float"\par
This format available with unity 2019.x+. Visual quality will suffer a little bit.\par
\par
2) You can \b decrease height of decals\b0 . The decals is drawn in the form of a cube. Smaller the height of the cube -> the less pixels are drawn.\par
Open blood prefab -> select decal -> change transform "y"\par
\par
3) You \b can remove shadows and depth writing\b0 . With directional light and depth rendering you can decrease number of triangles x4 times!\par
Open BFX_Blood shader -> find ths lines "//start remove line " and "//end remove light"\par
Remove the code from the "start" to "end" lines. (150 -> 231 line number)\par
\par
4) \b Use lower resolution \b0 for mobile devices :)\par
Do you really need to render the game in 2-4k on screen with size 5-7 inches?? Even not many pc games can work in 4k. I find this unnecessary.\par
For example use half resolution = 4x faster!\par
Screen.SetResolution((int)(Screen.width * 0.5f), (int)(Screen.height * 0.5f), FullScreenMode.ExclusiveFullScreen, 60);\par
---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------\par
\par
\ul\b Effect settings:\par
\ulnone\b0\par
For \cf5\ul\b scaling\ulnone\b0   \cf0 just change a prefab transform scale.\par
\par
You can \ul\b draw decals for only some layers\ulnone\b0 .\par
For that just add the script "BFX_BloodDecalLayers" on the main camera and select a layers for decal drawing.\par
You can change the "decal rendering mode" to draw or ignore the selected layers.\par
But this method has overhead since the selected layers need to be drawn in the depth buffer. You can optimize it (with a small artefacts) using smaller texture size. Just change the "LayerDepthResoulution" property.\par
\par
\par
Each prefab of a blood (blood1 - blood 15) have the attached script "BFX_BloodSettings"\par
\par
1) \ul\b\lang1033 AnimationSpeed\ulnone\b0\lang9\par
Speed of a fluid animation and a decal appearing animation.\par
\par
2)  \ul\b GroundHeight\par
\ulnone\b0 You should set the start height of a blood decal. It's your ground/floor/terrain world height position (transform -> position.y). The blood decal will be projected at this point.\par
I can't find the correct ground/floor height because some object doesn't have a collider.\par
In other cases (when you use a character/monster with a collider) I can't find a correct height because the "raycast" can intersect the monster collider instead of floor collider.\par
So you need to set this height manually.\par
In some cases you can use the "layer" mask for project decals on the floor/ground only (without monster/characters/..., etc). And then you can just increase the scale of "bloodPrefab -> decal" (transform -> scale.y).\par
For example instead of the default 2 meters you can use 6 meters or more. In this case the decal will be projected \~to world height position from -3 to 3 meters.\par
\par
3) \ul\b LightIntensityMultiplier \ulnone\b0 [0 - 1]\par
Brightness intensity  of blood and light glare. For day light use 1, for night use 0, etc.\par
\par
4) \ul\b FreezeDecalDisappearance\ulnone\b0\par
Default animation of blood (include decal fadeout) ~15 second.\par
You can stop(freeze) the disappearance animation of decal. if necessary, you can continue the animation, for example after 100 seconds, just by unfreezing the animation.\par
\par
\par
5) \ul\b DecalRenderingMode \ulnone\b0 (floorXZ/AverageRay)\par
By default, decal works as a projector. In most cases, it is sufficient to project a decal onto the floor along the XZ axis.\par
But sometimes it's necessary to project a decal onto the walls and floor in the same time. In this case, you can use the average angle between floor up vector and blood forward vector.\par
This method requires more resources for projection, so more pixel shader calculations are required. I recommend use this method with caution.\par
\par

